  <p>The 1440 upgrade is worth a sentence of its own. Three sources describe it as opened in 2025 and favored since, with 800 by 800 still displaying. That makes it an option rather than a floor, and nothing in the pool supports the traffic claims attached to it, which come from sellers of image production.</p>

  <h2>The five slots, and the white one</h2>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Nine of eighteen sources give the same main image count: five per listing, with the fifth reserved for a pure-white-background image in general categories. Eight of eighteen place the white image in the fifth slot specifically. Category standards move it: reproductions of the food and large-appliance standards both put the white image in the second slot.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: eighteen independent published specifications collected 10 September 2026, Chinese-language first, counted by domain rather than by page. Modal value with its agreement count, not a reading from the platform.</p>

  <p>The white-background image is not just the fifth photograph. It is a separate deliverable, briefed and checked on its own terms.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The white-background image is published as a separate deliverable with its own limits: pure white at RGB 255 255 255 on nine of eighteen sources, 800 by 800 pixels on six, and a file under 300KB on the four sources that give the slot its own weight cap. Three of those four also state a 38KB floor, which is a compression check rather than a design target.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: eighteen independent published specifications collected 10 September 2026, sixteen Chinese-language and two English-language, counted by domain. Modal values with their agreement counts, not a reading from the platform.</p>

  <p>How much of that white frame the product should fill is where the sources stop agreeing.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Published guidance on how much of the white-background frame the product should fill does not settle on one number. Four of eighteen sources give 70 to 80 percent, three more give a flat figure of about 70 percent, and the low end of the published spread is 60 percent. Nothing in the pool puts it outside 60 to 80 percent, and nothing in the pool states where the number came from.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: eighteen independent published specifications collected 10 September 2026, in Chinese and English, reported as the spread rather than averaged. Contested modal value, not a reading from the platform.</p>

  <p>A band of 60 to 80 percent with no stated origin is a working range, not a rule. Aim for the middle of it and confirm against a listing that has already passed.</p>

  <p>Taken together, the white-background image has its own checklist, and it is worth running separately from the other four mains.</p>

