  <h2>File weight, where the conflict turns out not to be one</h2>

  <p>Two figures circulate, 3MB and 500KB, and they are usually presented as sources contradicting each other. They are not.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The two figures that circulate for Tmall main image weight are not in conflict. Every source that publishes both treats 3MB as the platform cap and keeping the file under 500KB as a load-speed recommendation. Six of eighteen sources give the 3MB cap. Three of eighteen give the 500KB recommendation, and all three frame it as advice rather than as a limit.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: eighteen independent published specifications collected 10 September 2026, in Chinese and English, with the cap and the recommendation counted separately. Modal values with their agreement counts, not a reading from the platform.</p>

  <p>One number is a ceiling and the other is advice about staying well under it. Export under 500KB and you satisfy both readings at once.</p>

  <h2>Detail page width, mobile against desktop</h2>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Every source that separates desktop from mobile gives the same two detail page widths: 790 pixels for desktop, 750 pixels for mobile. Five of eighteen sources address the split, and all five agree. What they do not agree on is the weight and height of each slice, where published caps run from 200KB to 3MB and from 1500 to 2000 pixels tall.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: eighteen independent published specifications collected 10 September 2026, Chinese-language first. Modal values with their agreement counts, not a reading from the platform.</p>

  <p>Widths settle and slices do not. One domain gives three different slice heights across its own pages, which says more about the state of published guidance than any single figure could. Cut slices no taller than 1500 pixels and no heavier than 500KB and you sit inside every published cap.</p>

  <h2>Why did Tmall reject my product image?</h2>

  <p>No rejection notice was obtained for this page, and none is reconstructed here. What the sources do agree on is the list of things not to put on a main image, and it is the most consistent row in the whole pool.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Eleven of eighteen sources publish the same prohibition list for main images: no watermarks, no borders, no spliced composites, no promotional overlay text, no QR codes, no third-party logos, and no model or body part in the white-background slot. They do not agree on how much overlay text is tolerable elsewhere, where the published caps sit at 15 percent and 30 percent of frame area on one source each.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: eighteen independent published specifications collected 10 September 2026, Chinese-language first, counted by domain. Modal position with its agreement count, not a reading from the platform.</p>

  <p>Those are the prohibitions most often described, not a ranking of why listings actually fail, because no source ranks them. Superlative and absolute claims belong on the same list, and three sources note that the rule there comes from advertising law rather than from the platform alone.</p>

  <h2>Category variation, the part nobody translates</h2>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Image rules move by category, and the movement is in the slot map rather than the pixel count. Reproductions of two category standards put the white-background image in the second slot, not the fifth: the food standard follows it with a legible label image showing ingredients and licence number, and the large-appliance standard follows it with the compulsory certification mark and then the energy label. A ratio change dated September 2024 moved roughly 70 categories to square-first display while about ten, including womenswear and watches, held a 3:4 display.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: published Chinese category standards and a dated platform change report, collected 10 September 2026, one source per specific slot map and four of eighteen sources confirming that category variation exists at all. Contested for each specific map, not a reading from the platform.</p>

  <p>The general five-slot model is the default, not a universal. If you sell food, appliances, apparel or watches, check your own category standard before planning the carousel.</p>

  <h2>Do Tmall Global listings use different image rules?</h2>

  <p>Possibly, and nobody has published enough to say how.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">There is no published Tmall Global image delta to work from. Sixteen of eighteen sources carrying a Tmall image figure do not distinguish the cross-border channel from the domestic one at any point. The two that do give one figure each, each on a single source: a 15-image maximum with a 1600 by 1600 minimum, and a cross-border cosmetics slot map of five images plus one video. Neither is corroborated anywhere else in the pool.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: eighteen independent published specifications collected 10 September 2026, in Chinese and English, counted for whether they distinguish the two channels. Stated as an absence with its count, not as a platform rule.</p>

  <p>A delta table would need at least two sources agreeing on a figure. There are none, so this page publishes the absence rather than two single-source numbers dressed up as a comparison.</p>

  <h2>If you are also selling on JD</h2>

  <p>The <a href="/resources/insights/jd-image-requirements-vs-tmall">article comparing JD image requirements with Tmall</a> covers the one difference between the two that holds up, where the white-background image sits, and the detail-width difference that turns out to be a sourcing artifact. The <a href="/services/design/ecommerce">ecommerce design service page</a> covers producing the full marketplace image set. The <a href="/solutions/retailers">retailers solutions page</a> covers running one catalog across both platforms.</p>

  <p>Reviewed 10 September 2026. Confirm every figure in your own seller backend before building a batch to it.</p>

